# Build context for backend/wealdrelay/Dockerfile. The context is the repository
# root, because the Cargo workspace root lives there.
#
# Everything that is not needed to compile the relay is excluded, both for build
# speed and so that a stray local artifact cannot change the image digest.

.git
.github
.claude

# The macOS app. Nothing in the relay build touches it.
Sources
Tests
UITests
Weald.xcodeproj
project.yml
*.xcodeproj
weald-qa
build-evidence
scripts
specs
docs

# The prebuilt MLS xcframework. This is the same crate as backend/weald-mls,
# already compiled for macOS and vendored for the app to link against, and at
# ~100MB it was by some distance the largest thing reaching the builder. The
# relay compiles weald-mls from source; a macOS static library cannot
# participate in a Linux build and was only ever ballast.
Frameworks

# Rendered documents, agent skills and the git hooks. None of it compiles the
# relay. `output` in particular holds PDFs that are regenerated by hand, so its
# presence made the context digest depend on when somebody last rebuilt a
# whitepaper.
output
skills
.githooks
.gitignore

# The root LICENSE and NOTICE, where a repository has them. Apache 2.0 section 4
# is served by backend/wealdrelay/LICENSE and its NOTICE, which the Dockerfile
# copies into the image explicitly; a second copy at the root is for humans
# reading the repository and must not be a digest input. Excluded here rather
# than only in the public repository, so both contexts hash the same.
LICENSE
NOTICE

# Node, for the control plane and the marketing site. The relay is Rust only,
# and the only two workspace members its build needs are wealdrelay itself and
# weald-mls.
backend/weald-cloud
backend/weald-web
backend/compose
**/node_modules

# Local build output. `target` in particular is large and host-specific.
target
**/target
.build
DerivedData
build
node_modules
*.profraw

# Local state and editor noise. Everything here is host state, and host state
# inside the context is a reproducibility defect rather than merely waste: the
# Dockerfile ends with `COPY . .`, so a file that reaches the context reaches a
# layer and changes the published digest (step 13, and
# specs/backend/relay/verification.md proof 1).
.weald
.weald-dev
.weald-fixture
.weald-stack
.jax
tmp
.DS_Store
*.swp
.env
.env.*
.mcp.json

# The macOS side of the product and its assets. None of it compiles the relay.
Companion
Resources
icon-drafts
baselines
node_modules
package.json
package-lock.json
render.yaml
ExportOptions.plist
Weald.entitlements
Weald-Info.plist
*.md
.node-version
.gitattributes

# The relay's own deployment bundle. Templates, compose files and the systemd
# unit are shipped to operators, not compiled into the binary, and a doc edit
# must not change the image digest.
backend/wealdrelay/deploy

# The root `Dockerfile` is a symlink to backend/wealdrelay/Dockerfile. It exists
# because a provider that builds from a git repository looks for a Dockerfile at
# the repository root and offers no way to say otherwise (Render's create API and
# its MCP tools both take a repo and a runtime and nothing else). Excluded from
# the context so the one real file stays the only input to the digest: a symlink
# in the context would hash as a second copy of the same bytes and make the
# context digest depend on how the checkout materialised links.
# Written without a leading slash: Docker strips one and this file's own
# reimplementation in scripts/repro-context-digest.py does not, and the two have
# to agree or the context digest stops describing the context.
Dockerfile

# Research notebooks, renders and their virtualenvs. None of it compiles the
# relay, and two things made its presence in the context actively harmful: a
# virtualenv churns on every use, so the context digest moved while a build was
# running, and `.venv/bin/` holds symlinks, one of them to a Python outside this
# tree. Step 13's copy-of-the-context check is what found it, by refusing to
# build a copy that did not hash the same as the original.
research
**/.venv
